{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>The name of the one was Gershom, for he said: \"a stranger\" etc., and the name of the one was Eliezer, for the Elohim of my father was my help</b> etc. And here it is not written כי אמר — one might say כי אמר refers above, to Gershom, concerning whom it is written כי אמר. For there is a midrashic interpretation: when Jethro gave his daughter to Moses, he needed to stipulate that the firstborn son be for idolatry, and therefore he was not circumcised until the angel met him on the way and sought to kill him, and then Zipporah circumcised him and annulled the stipulation that had been made with her father. Therefore here it is written כי אמר — i.e., now it was revealed to all that he was coerced by this stipulation, for he was a stranger and needed to fulfill Jethro's word. Nevertheless, through this stipulation Jonathan emerged from him, who became a priest for Micah's idol. Therefore כי אמר is not written concerning Eliezer, for he did not wish to reveal that he had killed a soul and Pharaoh wanted to kill him. But in the Gemara it is implied that Eliezer son of Moses was not circumcised until the angel met him — for Moses said: I will circumcise and go out on the road, [which] is dangerous for an infant, and this was Eliezer, for when he came to Egypt we do not find that he had a son afterward."], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase לע\"א is an abbreviation; resolved here as לעבודת אלילים (for idolatry) based on the midrashic context of Jonathan son of Gershom becoming priest to Micah's idol (Judges 17–18). The word מחלה is read as מחלה לו (annulled for him) per the rabbinic tradition in Shabbat 134b."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:13:34.394016712Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}